An assistant chat card - header, conversation, a "thinking" status, suggestion chips and a composer. A **block**:
a ready-made composition of the UI primitives (Avatar, Spinner, Button, Icon), like the cards on the
[Appica homepage](https://appica.dev/ui).

<%= embed Blocks::ChatAssistantPreview, :default %>

## Blocks

Primitives (`UI::`) are the building bricks; blocks (`Blocks::`) compose them into product-level pieces. Each block
lives in its own folder with its parts, Stimulus controller and preview - the same sidecar layout as the
primitives - and is a `Phlex::Kit`, so a view that does `include Blocks` calls it like a tag:

```
app/components/blocks/chat_assistant/
  chat_assistant.rb  chat_header.rb  chat_messages.rb  chat_message.rb
  chat_status.rb  chat_suggestions.rb  chat_suggestion.rb  chat_composer.rb
  chat_assistant_controller.js  chat_assistant_preview.rb
```

A block owns layout and data wiring. When it needs something reusable, that becomes a primitive.

## Usage

```ruby
class ChatPanel < Phlex::HTML
  include Blocks

  def view_template
    ChatAssistant(title: "Appica Assistant", action: chat_messages_path, messages_id: "chat-messages") do
      ChatHeader()
      ChatMessages do
        @messages.each { |message| ChatMessage(from: message.role) { message.body } }
        ChatStatus(hidden: true)
      end
      ChatSuggestions([ "Add a FAQ section", "Use dark theme" ])
      ChatComposer(placeholder: "Ask anything…")
    end
  end
end
```

Host app: `import "blocks"` in `application.js` after `import "ui"`.

## Replying from the server

The composer is a real `<form>` posting `message` to `action:`. When it's sent, the controller shows the user's bubble
straight away, clears the field and shows the status row. Answer with a Turbo Stream that appends the assistant's
bubble to the messages list. The controller notices the new message, hides the status and scrolls to it.

```ruby
# app/controllers/chat_messages_controller.rb
def create
  reply = Assistant.reply_to(params[:message])   # or enqueue a job and broadcast later
  render turbo_stream: turbo_stream.append("chat-messages", Blocks::ChatMessage.new(from: :assistant) { reply })
end
```

For streamed answers, broadcast the same append from a job (`Turbo::StreamsChannel.broadcast_append_to`).

## Localization

The block's own strings - the default title, the badge's "Online"/"Offline", the conversation and composer labels,
the placeholder, the "Thinking…" status and the demo reply - are translated. They live next to the component in
`chat_assistant.locales.yml` (English, Czech, German and Arabic side by side, under
`ui_components.blocks.chat_assistant`). Anything you pass (`title:`, `placeholder:`, messages, suggestions) is shown
as-is. Switch the **Locale** display option in the preview toolbar to try it - the previews' sample conversation is
translated too (`chat_assistant_preview.locales.yml`, loaded only in Lookbook).

